import{_ as a,o as n,c as t,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.mm2kvo8B.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Sampling","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/sampling.md","filePath":"guides/sampling.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"guides/sampling.md"};function r(s,e,d,c,p,l){return n(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="Sampling" tabindex="-1">Sampling <a class="header-anchor" href="#Sampling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sampling {#Sampling}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Choose an initial state, run independent chains, and interpret the returned positions without confusing them with warm-up state.</p><h2 id="Target" tabindex="-1">Target <a class="header-anchor" href="#Target" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Target {#Target}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The installation command on <a href="./../">Start</a> adds the checked-out revision and its unregistered dependencies to Julia&#39;s active environment. Use <code>julia --project=path/to/your/environment WarmupHMC.jl/scripts/install.jl</code> to choose a project explicitly. The script reads dependency revisions from the package&#39;s <code>Project.toml</code> rather than relying on Julia&#39;s transitive handling of <code>[sources]</code> tables.</p><p>A target implements <code>LogDensityProblems.dimension</code>, <code>capabilities</code>, <code>logdensity</code>, and <code>logdensity_and_gradient</code>. The last method returns a scalar log density and its gradient in the same coordinates. Additive constants in the density cancel. The <a href="./../examples/gaussian">Gaussian example</a> supplies an analytic gradient; an automatic differentiation wrapper can provide the same interface.</p><p>The dimension is the length of the unconstrained parameter vector. Transform constrained parameters before sampling and include the corresponding Jacobian in the log density. A finite starting log density and gradient are required.</p><h2 id="Initialization" tabindex="-1">Initialization <a class="header-anchor" href="#Initialization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Initialization {#Initialization}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>adaptive_warmup_mcmc(rng, problem; ...)</code> uses Pathfinder by default. Use <code>init</code> to supply a known position or a position with a known scale. A scale is an operator mapping a standard normal vector into a displacement in parameter space; it is not a covariance matrix. See the <code>init</code> argument in <a href="/WarmupHMC.jl/dev/reference/sampling#WarmupHMC.adaptive_warmup_mcmc"><code>adaptive_warmup_mcmc</code></a> for the accepted forms and stationary-start controls.</p><p>Request retained draws with <code>n_draws</code>. Warm-up may restart and discard an earlier sampling epoch; the returned positions belong to the retained epoch. Treat the requested count as a floor, and inspect the actual matrix shape. Set <code>progress=nothing</code> in batch scripts and <code>monitor_ess=true</code> when the run should compute ESS without a progress display. The random number generator is explicit, which allows independent seeds and avoids reliance on the process-global RNG.</p><h2 id="Independent-chains" tabindex="-1">Independent chains <a class="header-anchor" href="#Independent-chains" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Independent chains {#Independent-chains}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The vector-of-RNGs overload runs independent adaptive chains and returns a vector of results. Give each chain its own RNG. A shared problem is copied where needed for adaptation; an explicitly supplied vector of problems remains the caller&#39;s responsibility to make independent. Mutable reparametrization state must not be shared between concurrently running chains.</p><p><a href="/WarmupHMC.jl/dev/reference/sampling#WarmupHMC.cooperative_warmup_mcmc"><code>cooperative_warmup_mcmc</code></a> instead shares adaptation evidence across chains. <a href="/WarmupHMC.jl/dev/reference/sampling#WarmupHMC.completion_warmup_mcmc"><code>completion_warmup_mcmc</code></a> adds a completion policy to independent chains. These are different sampling policies, not interchangeable ways to display a progress bar. In particular, an early completion rule can select chains by runtime; assess that selection when runtime depends on posterior location.</p><h2 id="Returned-coordinates" tabindex="-1">Returned coordinates <a class="header-anchor" href="#Returned-coordinates" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Returned coordinates {#Returned-coordinates}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>posterior_position</code> stores coordinates in rows and draws in columns. For a <code>ReparametrizedProblem</code>, these positions have already been transformed into the wrapped model&#39;s coordinates. Do not transform them again. <code>posterior_gradient</code>, <code>halo_position</code>, <code>halo_gradient</code>, and the saved active position retain working coordinates, so they cannot be paired with returned model-frame positions as though they shared a frame. The reported <code>ess</code> belongs to the returned positions.</p><p>Use <code>n_divergent_samples</code> and effective sample size to inspect the retained sample. Compare multiple independently initialized chains for convergence; single-chain ESS cannot establish agreement between chains or discovery of separated modes. <code>sampling_evaluation_counter</code> counts gradient evaluations for retained sampling transitions. <code>total_evaluation_counter</code> also includes work from discarded epochs and adaptation, but excludes Pathfinder and initial step-size search. Neither counter is elapsed time.</p><p>The <a href="./../examples/gaussian">Gaussian calibration example</a> reads the matrix shape, moments, ESS, divergence count, and evaluation counters from one run.</p>',16)])])}const u=a(o,[["render",r]]);export{h as __pageData,u as default};
